  [34mexport[39m [34mfunction[39m [33mclearSession[39m()                     [32m// removes the key[39m

  - One namespaced key, [38;5;153mappSession[39m, holding JSON. Namespacing avoids collisions with anything else on the origin.
  - [38;5;153mgetSession()[39m never throws: a missing key, malformed JSON, or an object lacking [38;5;153muserId[39m all return [38;5;153mnull[39m. Corrupt
    storage means "logged out", not a crashed page. This is the defensive edge the rest of the app gets to stop worrying
    about.
  - [38;5;153msetSession()[39m rejects a missing or non-string [38;5;153muserId[39m — writing a broken record is worse than not writing one, because
    it persists.
  - Writes are wrapped for [38;5;153mQuotaExceededError[39m and for Safari private-mode, where [38;5;153mlocalStorage[39m access can throw. A failed
    write degrades to an in-memory-only session for that page rather than breaking login.

  [1m[38;5;153mapp.js[0m — [38;5;153mlogin()[39m changes contract rather than signature:

  [34masync[39m [34mfunction[39m [33mlogin[39m(username, password)   [32m// → { success: true, userId, username }[39m
                                             [32m//   { success: false, error }[39m

  Two deliberate points here:

  - [1mThe signature keeps [38;5;153m(username, password)[39m.[0m The [38;5;153muserId[39m comes back from the auth response, as you specified — it's in
    the return value, not a parameter. This is the one place the design departs from the literal wording of your
    original request, and it's because a caller can't supply an identity it hasn't authenticated yet.
  - [1m[38;5;153mlogin()[39m becomes [38;5;153masync[39m now, while it's still a stub.[0m A real [38;5;153mfetch[39m to [38;5;153mAPI_ENDPOINT[39m forces this eventually, and
    converting a sync function to async later breaks every call site silently — callers get a [38;5;153mPromise[39m where they
    expected an object, and [38;5;153mresult.success[39m is [38;5;153mundefined[39m, which is falsy, so the failure is quiet. Paying the one [38;5;153mawait[39m
    today costs nothing. The stub keeps returning a fabricated [38;5;153muserId[39m until the backend exists.

  [1m[38;5;153mindex.html[0m — [38;5;153m<script src="app.js">[39m becomes [38;5;153m<script type="module" src="app.js">[39m. This is what requires serving over
  HTTP rather than [38;5;153mfile://[39m.

  [1m[38;5;153msrc/utils.js[39m / [38;5;153msrc/index.js[0m stay untouched on CommonJS. They're the Node side, they don't interact with [38;5;153mapp.js[39m, and
  converting them is scope creep.

  Does that structure look right before I go on to data flow, error handling, and testing?

